1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,440
We found their tools.

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:03,080
Where are their clothes?

3
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:04,880
Picture a riverbank.

4
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:07,120
More than a million years ago, someone

5
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:08,800
picks up a stone and chips away

6
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:11,360
at it until one edge becomes sharp.

7
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:14,000
Water, wind and time will wear away

8
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:15,920
almost everything around it.

9
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,400
But that stone will survive.

10
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,480
Thousands of generations pass.

11
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,320
New people come to the river, bringing

12
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:24,960
new tools with them: bone needles, shaped

13
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:26,960
by careful human hands.

14
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,120
Stone and bone outlast the people who

15
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:31,520
made them. Almost all their clothing is

16
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:34,400
gone. But not quite everything.

17
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,800
Here’s a tiny sandal, woven from esparto

18
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,840
grass. A living person once wore it.

19
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:42,240
Imagine them pulling the straps tight, stepping

20
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:44,240
outside, and heading off to whatever the

21
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:45,760
day had in store.

22
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:48,080
Thousands of years later, the sandal is

23
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:51,040
still here. Suddenly, the past has more

24
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:53,120
than tools. It has feet.

25
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:55,520
Follow its owner's footsteps, and we reach

26
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:57,600
the other bank: Roman Britain.

27
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:01,080
Tunics, cloaks, belts, shoes.

28
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:03,520
Now we're not looking at isolated traces.

29
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:05,920
We're looking at people, dressed and ready

30
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,600
to go about their lives.

31
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:09,600
One look gives way to another, and

32
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:11,440
the river carries us on.

33
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:14,080
By around 1400, the silhouettes have changed

34
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:18,000
again. Long garments, belts, head coverings.

35
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:20,080
Straight lines give way to folds and

36
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:22,480
layers. Even from a distance, you can

37
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,880
see how different a passerby looks.

38
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:28,040
Now it's Amsterdam, 1559.

39
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,560
A family portrait.

40
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:31,920
Look closely: these people belong to the

41
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,080
same family, but each has a style

42
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:35,160
of their own.

43
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,280
Different hats, fabrics, little details.

44
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:40,240
One painting, and a whole family of

45
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:42,320
strangers looking straight at us across the

46
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:44,920
centuries. Which would you choose?

47
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:46,800
The river carries us into the eighteenth

48
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:49,880
century. A French dress spreads wide.

49
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:52,480
Beside it, a British court outfit: fine

50
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,653
decoration, a waistcoat, a long coat.

51
00:01:55,707 --> 00:01:57,600
You wouldn't exactly blend into the background

52
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,160
wearing either of these.

53
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:00,880
Walk into a room, and your outfit

54
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:02,680
arrives before you do.

55
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:04,920
But wide is not the limit.

56
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:07,280
In the nineteenth century, the crinoline appears

57
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:08,560
beneath the skirt.

58
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:10,600
Suddenly, a person is surrounded by a

59
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:12,480
whole dome of fabric.

60
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:14,373
Imagine trying to get through a doorway,

61
00:02:14,427 --> 00:02:15,600
or sitting next to someone in a

62
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:18,293
carriage. Now let's jump ahead.

63
00:02:18,347 --> 00:02:21,760
The 1920s. In some dresses, that enormous

64
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,840
dome gives way to a completely different

65
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:27,000
shape: long, straight and loose.

66
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:28,640
Like a broad wave on the river

67
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,360
suddenly smoothing out.

68
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,053
One more step, and we're on a

69
00:02:32,187 --> 00:02:35,200
modern street. Jeans and dresses, sneakers and

70
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,680
tailored suits, all side by side.

71
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:40,240
We wear these things every day, hardly

72
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:42,160
thinking that one day they'll be history

73
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:45,080
too. Picture a museum in the future.

74
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:48,480
Behind the glass: our sneakers, a jacket,

75
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:50,040
someone's favorite T-shirt.

76
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:52,560
Visitors study them as closely as we

77
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:54,000
study that ancient sandal.

78
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:56,480
Maybe they wonder who wore them, and

79
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:57,360
where they were going.

80
00:02:57,920 --> 00:02:59,440
The river keeps flowing.

81
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:03,440
Things survive, disappear, and surprise people centuries

82
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:05,760
later. We found their tools.

83
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,600
What will they find after us?
